 Trent shares jump 13% to 2-year high on bullish brokerage calls after strong Q2 update; should you buy, sell, or hold?
 Trent shares rallied following a strong Q2FY27 operational update that exceeded market expectations.
 Paras Bisht 
 October 06, 2026 / 14:08 IST 
 --> 
 Trent shares rise in trade. 
 Trent shares jumped nearly 13 percent on Tuesday and were on track for their best session since August 2024, after the Tata Group retailer reported strong revenue growth for the September quarter and brokerages turned positive on its growth outlook.
The stock hit an intraday high of Rs 2,909 on the NSE, up 12.75 percent.
Analysts attributed the sharp rise to improving sales productivity despite rapid store expansion across Trent's key formats, including Westside and Zudio.
The company added 250 stores across its Westside and Zudio formats in fiscal 2026 and opened another 27 stores in the quarter ended September 30.
Trent reported a 23 percent year-on-year rise in standalone revenue to Rs 5,788 crore in the second quarter. Revenue for the first half of FY27 rose 21 percent to Rs 11,454 crore. The company also crossed the 1,000-store milestone for Zudio during the quarter and had 1,342 stores across its retail portfolio as of September 30.
Mayank Jain, Market Analyst, Share.Market by PhonePe, said the sharp price movement on the daily chart represented an initial attempt at trend recovery following a multi-month decline from peak levels.
The daily gain of more than 11 percent took the stock above its 20-day Simple Moving Average at 2,740.6 and 200-day Simple Moving Average at 2,774.4, with the stock trading at Rs 2,864.90 along with higher volumes.
"Price is now directly testing dynamic resistance at the 50-day Simple Moving Average of 2,871.4, which marks an immediate technical boundary before higher overhead levels near Rs 3,000 to Rs 3,200, which may act as the next resistance zone," he said.
"On the downside, if the stock fails to hold the support cluster between the 20-day and 200-day moving averages near Rs 2,740 to Rs 2,775, the primary support zone according to the chart rests in the Rs 2,500 to Rs 2,600 area," Jain added.
Analysts said the revenue growth was accompanied by signs that the expansion was not coming at the expense of store productivity.
Uttam Kumar Srimal, Deputy Head - Fundamental Research, Axis Direct, said Trent's shares rallied following a strong Q2FY27 operational update that exceeded market expectations.
"The primary catalyst was a 23 percent YoY surge in standalone revenue to Rs 5,788 crore, bringing total H1FY27 top-line growth to 21 percent YoY at Rs 11,454 crore. Aggressive footprint expansion in Zudio served as the core growth engine, with the value-fashion chain surpassing the 1,000-store milestone and pushing Trent's total retail network to 1,342 outlets," he said.
"Crucially, revenue momentum accelerated despite persistent macroeconomic headwinds around discretionary retail spending. Underlying operational efficiency also improved, as the drop in store-level revenue productivity narrowed significantly to 1.7 percent in Q2, down from a 5.6 percent contraction in Q1," Srimal added.
He said whether the sharp share price move translates into a structural stock re-rating or remains a brief rise will depend on the upcoming full Q2 earnings, particularly operating margins, bottom-line profitability and same-store sales growth.
Jigar S. Patel, Senior Manager of Equity Technical Research at Anand Rathi Share and Stock Brokers, said the stock's hourly chart showed a shift in the Relative Strength Index from a bearish to a bullish setup.
"After nearly a month, RSI has moved back above the 70 level, indicating a strong improvement in short-term momentum and renewed buying interest. This shift suggests that the stock may continue its recovery and sustain the positive momentum in the near term," Patel said.
"However, considering the recent upmove, investors should prefer a buy-on-dips strategy rather than chasing the stock at higher levels. The Rs 2,700 zone will act as a major support, and holding this level would keep the bullish structure intact. On the upside, the stock is expected to gradually move towards Rs 3,000," he added.
Brokerages also highlighted the improvement in sales productivity.
Goldman Sachs said sales productivity improved sequentially, with revenue per store declining 1.7 percent in the second quarter compared with a 5.6 percent fall in the previous quarter. It said revenue growth exceeded its estimates.
Macquarie said momentum in same-store sales growth likely improved in the second quarter from the first, while Citi said revenue exceeded its expectations and the decline in revenue per square foot was the slowest in five quarters.
Trent competes with value-focused retailers such as Vishal Mega Mart, V-Mart Retail, Style Bazaar and Reliance Trends, as well as premium brands including Shoppers Stop and H&M.
